WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.944 --> 00:00:03.888
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:20.025 --> 00:00:23.290
My father said, I ought
to go to Boston Tech.

00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:25.750
And there was a kid
named Tom Peterson

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:28.910
who went east to Harvard.

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:32.369
And when he came over in
the summer, I said Tom,

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:35.570
did you ever hear
of Boston Tech?

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:36.790
Well, he said, no.

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:40.670
They changed the name,
they moved it from Boston

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:44.150
over to Cambridge, and they
called the Massachusetts

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:46.047
Institute of Technology.

00:00:46.047 --> 00:00:47.300
[CHEERING]

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:52.010
And then he looked me right
in the eye and said real loud,

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:55.012
they only take smart ones!

00:00:55.012 --> 00:00:56.374
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:01.400
I learned from that not to ever
believe anything that a Harvard

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:03.338
person tells you.

00:01:03.338 --> 00:01:04.805
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:11.360
Well, it seems like yesterday
I came over here in 1926

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:12.914
to look over MIT.

00:01:12.914 --> 00:01:14.330
I had been working
in Schenectady,

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:16.450
New York at the General
Electric company,

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:18.490
and was admitted as
a graduate student

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:20.830
in electrical engineering
to work on motors.

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:23.050
It was during the course
of his graduate studies

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:25.660
in electrical engineering
that Doc designed

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:27.310
and developed the strobe.

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:31.210
He needed a way to observe parts
that moved so quickly that they

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:33.940
were a blur, and it
was for this purpose

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:36.240
that he invented something new--

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:39.580
an incredibly brief,
controllable, and repeatable

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:41.250
flash.

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:43.180
I worked for three
years on motors,

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:45.460
and I suddenly
realized, hey, there's

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:47.161
a lot of things in
the world that move.

00:01:47.161 --> 00:01:49.285
And I looked around, there
was a faucet [INAUDIBLE]

00:01:49.285 --> 00:01:50.754
right next to where I worked.

00:01:50.754 --> 00:01:53.170
So I just moved the strobe
over and took pictures of water

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:54.826
coming out of the faucet.

00:01:54.826 --> 00:02:00.100
And that's the first picture I
ever took, except for a motor.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:02.170
And Edgerton found that
there were other ways

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:04.180
to study motion.

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:08.830
By repeatedly flashing a strobe,
he could create multiple images

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:10.358
on a single photograph.

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:15.244
Now, there exists a
[INAUDIBLE] picture--

00:02:15.244 --> 00:02:17.160
whether the lights running
100 times a second,

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:19.520
I got two big powerful
strobe lights.

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:21.070
Well, here comes the club--

00:02:21.070 --> 00:02:23.070
see, it's accelerating,
and you can plot a curve

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:24.747
into the velocity
versus time, and you

00:02:24.747 --> 00:02:27.330
get the [INAUDIBLE] information
here [INAUDIBLE] the slightest

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:31.174
bit interested in, such is
the velocity of his tee.

00:02:31.174 --> 00:02:32.078
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:35.940
Now, this picture was a fizzle.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:38.456
I normally throw away
all my bad negatives,

00:02:38.456 --> 00:02:40.080
and I was just about
to throw this out,

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.910
and I thought, oh, I'll
make a print of that.

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:45.726
And it's down in the Museum
of Modern Art in New York.

00:02:45.726 --> 00:02:46.632
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:51.719
Supposed to be real art.

00:02:51.719 --> 00:02:54.260
I don't care-- that's all right,
if they want to call it art,

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:55.747
why don't you call it art.

00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:02.100
Well, I [INAUDIBLE] was more
of an electrical engineer,

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:04.230
because it's all done
with electricity.

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:06.180
You saw these [INAUDIBLE]
slides around here--

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:07.810
they're all batteries
and capacitors

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:08.850
and electronic lamps.

00:03:12.786 --> 00:03:14.262
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:33.440
I know there's somebody come
in here from MGM, and he says,

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:36.620
I want a movie of
throwing a custard pie

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:38.660
through an electric fan.

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:41.300
Well, I said, I'm a
serious scientist.

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:43.930
I'm not going to fool
with any custard pies.

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:49.220
Well, let's compromise on
an egg-- what do you say?

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:51.770
The film was called,
Quicker'n a Wink,

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:55.880
and it won a 1936
Academy Award for MGM.

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:58.400
I always thought that my
cat, in lapping her milk,

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:00.150
curled her tongue up.

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:04.170
But now it's revealed that
she curls her tongue down.

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:06.040
See?

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:07.690
In other words, she
brings the milk up

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:10.520
on the underside of her tongue.

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:14.170
Now, see how smart you get
when you go to the movies?

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:16.570
Years later, Doc
and his colleagues

00:04:16.570 --> 00:04:20.290
added the finishing touch,
matching slow-motion sound

00:04:20.290 --> 00:04:23.500
to high-speed pictures.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:24.970
[PHONE RINGING]

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:27.745
[BELLS RINGING]

00:04:27.745 --> 00:04:30.120
This was amazing--in the war,
when we were having trouble

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:32.619
with the Germans, and we wanted
to know what they were doing

00:04:32.619 --> 00:04:33.360
at night time.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:35.370
The year was 1938.

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:40.040
Doc and his associates went to
work on the top-secret project.

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:43.940
The end result was
57,000 watt second strobe

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:49.700
package, powered by 8 kilowatts,
and weighing 3,700 pounds.

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:52.430
The value of the aerial
strobe dramatically

00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:56.150
demonstrated the night before
the invasion of Normandy.

00:04:56.150 --> 00:04:59.660
The photos showed little or
no movement of enemy troops,

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:03.260
indicating that the Germans were
unprepared for the invasion.

00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:07.190
The clouds were down
about 1,000 feet,

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:08.940
and the flash bomb
couldn't use it at all,

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:12.110
because they were designed
to be working at 10,000 feet.

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:13.920
So those pictures were useful.

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:15.660
They were used all
during the war.

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:17.530
The Atomic Energy
Commission asked

00:05:17.530 --> 00:05:21.090
Edgerton and his associates,
Germeshausen and Greer,

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:25.290
to form a company,
which later became EG&amp;G,

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:29.850
and to design a testing camera
to analyze nuclear explosions.

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:31.440
And then suddenly
one day, we had

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:34.050
an agreement with
the Russians not

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:36.210
to have any more atom bombs.

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:39.270
All my friends came around here
and said oh, that's too bad,

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:41.102
your company is
going down the drain.

00:05:41.102 --> 00:05:42.810
Well, that was the
best year we ever had,

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:44.893
because all the people got
up early in the morning

00:05:44.893 --> 00:05:48.640
and started thinking
about other things to do.

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:51.820
And there was all
kinds of things to do.

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:54.880
Doc turned his strobe
to the undersea world,

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:58.100
where movement was
sometimes imperceptible.

00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:00.130
By having a movie
camera that takes

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:02.800
one picture every
20 seconds, and you

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:06.380
can take things that happen
slowly and speed them up.

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:09.844
Now, I was trying to get these
sand dollars to spell MIT.

00:06:09.844 --> 00:06:11.700
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:14.090
They're very poor learners,
very poor learners.

00:06:14.090 --> 00:06:15.542
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:15.542 --> 00:06:17.500
I asked him to bring some
of those sand dollars

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:20.287
back, because my grandson
wanted to see them.

00:06:20.287 --> 00:06:22.120
Actually, it was me
that wanted to see them,

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:23.710
but that's all right.

00:06:23.710 --> 00:06:25.335
They have little
round things-- they've

00:06:25.335 --> 00:06:27.950
got small feet on the bottom,
so they can walk around.

00:06:27.950 --> 00:06:28.830
Up, Eric, up!

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:29.780
Raise them up!

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:30.280
That a boy.

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:31.280
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:37.370
I got a telephone call that said
there was some unknown man come

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:39.762
in from France, named
Jacques Cousteau,

00:06:39.762 --> 00:06:40.970
and nobody ever heard of him.

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:43.670
But he's here, and could
he come up and talk to you

00:06:43.670 --> 00:06:45.560
about underwater photography?

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:48.990
So he came in, and I found out
he had invented the aqua lung.

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:51.860
And he hadn't been at MIT more
than a couple hours before we

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:53.990
had a tank on his
back, and he was

00:06:53.990 --> 00:06:58.770
testing my latest underwater
strobe in the swimming pool.

00:06:58.770 --> 00:07:02.080
And that led to many, many
expeditions on the Calypso,

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:05.010
and a lot of articles
in the Geographic.

00:07:05.010 --> 00:07:07.130
Edgerton found
that sonar could be

00:07:07.130 --> 00:07:09.520
used to make
pictures with sound,

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:13.220
just as photographs
are made with light.

00:07:13.220 --> 00:07:16.010
At Boston's Charles
River, he developed

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:20.510
and tested a whole new
array of sonar devices.

00:07:20.510 --> 00:07:25.040
One called a boomer, or pinger,
penetrates the underwater mud

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:28.070
to reveal objects below.

00:07:28.070 --> 00:07:30.430
Another of Edgerton's
sonar inventions

00:07:30.430 --> 00:07:34.040
is a side scanner, used to
locate objects which project

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:37.060
above the bottom of the sea.

00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:40.640
I was in Athens with
Cousteau on the Calypso,

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:43.040
and he came in one day
and said, is that side

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:45.260
scan sonar is good
for picking up

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:48.050
a ship that's 1,000 feet long?

00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:49.580
Think you can find it?

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:51.560
Well, I said, if I
can't, we better go home.

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:55.421
With a sonar, you ought to be
able to find it in no time.

00:07:55.421 --> 00:07:58.796
It was a long ways from
where anybody ever thought.

00:07:58.796 --> 00:07:59.792
[YELLING]

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:05.310
And there it was, just
as clear and sharp.

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:10.130
Hi, how are you?

00:08:10.130 --> 00:08:12.490
When MIT has its
open [INAUDIBLE],,

00:08:12.490 --> 00:08:15.930
Dr. Edgerton makes thousands of
copies of his famous milk drop

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:18.230
pictures, and gives
them away to what he

00:08:18.230 --> 00:08:20.450
hopes may be future students.

00:08:20.450 --> 00:08:22.760
The milk drop is probably
the best known picture

00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:24.920
Dr. Edgerton has ever taken.

00:08:24.920 --> 00:08:29.200
But he is equally well-known
as a superb teacher.

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:32.000
Now, we'll try to find
it with the strobe.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:35.179
And this disk is used in
music for tuning instruments.

00:08:35.179 --> 00:08:37.919
You swing in a microphone,
it changes the frequency

00:08:37.919 --> 00:08:40.789
of the light, you look at a disk
that's run by a constant feed

00:08:40.789 --> 00:08:45.330
motor, and you can tell
whether the girl's singing

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:47.760
flat or sharp, or
whatever it is.

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:49.956
Some people can tell
without this strobe.

00:08:49.956 --> 00:08:51.157
[LAUGHTER]

00:08:51.157 --> 00:08:53.240
If you have a particular
interest in these things,

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:55.700
we got here, come on
down and run them.

00:08:58.646 --> 00:09:00.119
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:04.540 --> 00:09:08.470
Several years ago, I heard
Jean Brown make a remark

00:09:08.470 --> 00:09:11.290
that she hadn't been married
to Gordon very long before she

00:09:11.290 --> 00:09:13.971
realized that he
had another love--

00:09:13.971 --> 00:09:14.470
MIT.

00:09:17.020 --> 00:09:21.180
I, too, had the same experience
when I married Harold.

00:09:21.180 --> 00:09:23.710
Unfortunately, it didn't
take me very long to become

00:09:23.710 --> 00:09:27.730
enamored with MIT, myself.

00:09:27.730 --> 00:09:32.053
And so we've been a happy
triangle ever since.

00:09:32.053 --> 00:09:33.937
[LAUGHTER]

00:09:33.937 --> 00:09:34.879
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:36.775 --> 00:09:38.650
One of the first miracles
that happened to me

00:09:38.650 --> 00:09:41.110
is when I was about
five years old,

00:09:41.110 --> 00:09:46.210
and my father brought home
a beautiful Jersey cow.

00:09:46.210 --> 00:09:48.310
And he let it in the
barn, and my sister

00:09:48.310 --> 00:09:52.720
and I went in after him,
and we were both given cups

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:55.570
by my mother, and we'd put
them under the faucet--

00:09:55.570 --> 00:09:57.070
[LAUGHTER]

00:09:57.070 --> 00:09:59.080
--and drink that warm
milk out of that cow.

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:01.540
It was really an
experience to remember.

00:10:01.540 --> 00:10:04.520
I also remember there was a
smart aleck kid from Omaha--

00:10:04.520 --> 00:10:05.750
that's a big town, you know?

00:10:05.750 --> 00:10:07.260
I live in a little town.

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:08.510
And he says, how does it work?

00:10:08.510 --> 00:10:12.240
Well, I say, you put hay
and water in one end,

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:15.010
and there's a kind of a bag
on the other with those four

00:10:15.010 --> 00:10:16.690
things that's
sticking down there.

00:10:16.690 --> 00:10:18.250
And he says, what are those for?

00:10:18.250 --> 00:10:20.450
Well, I said, one
of them is for milk,

00:10:20.450 --> 00:10:24.830
the other one's for skim
milk, another one's for cream.

00:10:24.830 --> 00:10:27.170
He says, what's
the other one for?

00:10:27.170 --> 00:10:30.340
I said, that's for
chocolate milk.

00:10:30.340 --> 00:10:32.280
You can see, even when
I was five years old,

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:35.030
I was an educator.

00:10:35.030 --> 00:10:36.690
Yeah, but you see,
my question is,

00:10:36.690 --> 00:10:40.170
even if this goes off in
one millionth of a second,

00:10:40.170 --> 00:10:42.030
if your shutter speed
is not that quick,

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:43.407
you're not going see it anyway.

00:10:43.407 --> 00:10:44.990
You don't use a
shatter in the camera.

00:10:44.990 --> 00:10:46.830
You do it in a
dark room, and you

00:10:46.830 --> 00:10:48.771
let all the exposure
come from the light.

00:10:48.771 --> 00:10:50.270
There you go, I
didn't realize that.

00:10:50.270 --> 00:10:51.519
You learned something tonight.

00:10:51.519 --> 00:10:52.587
Congratulations.

00:10:52.587 --> 00:10:53.928
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:55.716 --> 00:10:58.010
So there's a lot of
things I can't do,

00:10:58.010 --> 00:10:59.270
I just say, push that over.

00:10:59.270 --> 00:11:00.756
That's for the next generation.

00:11:00.756 --> 00:11:02.630
Finally this evening,
our person of the week.

00:11:02.630 --> 00:11:05.600
It is unusual for us to
choose someone who just died.

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:08.150
We do so this week because,
as often happens, only

00:11:08.150 --> 00:11:11.180
when someone dies does a
really large audience of people

00:11:11.180 --> 00:11:13.550
have come to realize
and appreciate

00:11:13.550 --> 00:11:15.890
how much that person
had accomplished.

00:11:15.890 --> 00:11:19.370
And we are all so much
more enlightened because

00:11:19.370 --> 00:11:21.680
of this man's imagination.

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:24.380
Doc Edgerton, the
scientist and teacher,

00:11:24.380 --> 00:11:28.040
who wanted to be remembered
as much for his views on life

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:31.340
as much as for the life
he enabled us to view.

00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:34.910
And so we choose Harold Doc
Edgerton, who has died at 86.

00:11:34.910 --> 00:11:37.430
Tinkerer and visionary,
he shed light

00:11:37.430 --> 00:11:38.960
where there was only shadow.

00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:41.570
He enlarged the world for
scientists, historians,

00:11:41.570 --> 00:11:43.820
and those of us just
with family scrapbooks.

00:11:43.820 --> 00:11:46.040
He was, most
personally, dedicated

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:49.610
to those thousands of people who
passed through his classroom.

00:11:49.610 --> 00:11:55.520
I'm just a crazy old professor,
he said, crazy like a fox.

00:11:55.520 --> 00:11:57.970
[MUSIC PLAYING]

